    presetHtml: `<p>Two thousand pixels give the portal's zoom something genuine to enlarge, so packaging copy and stitching stay legible when a guest inspects the detail view. Quality 80 is the balance point: rich enough for a white-sweep hero, restrained enough that the file rarely flirts with the 10 MB cap.</p>
<p>Treat these numbers as a dependable default rather than gospel — always re-check your current category help article in the partner portal, since some departments publish stricter rules than the platform-wide baseline.</p>`,
    deepH2: "Planograms, white sweeps, and the ten-megabyte guardrail",
    deepHtml: `<p>Retail teams burn hours when a single master gets forced across every surface. A Target Plus hero wants a centered 2000 square on white; a furniture supplier portal or a resale app expects entirely different framing and byte budgets.</p>
<p>Before you batch an entire assortment, compare the geometry with a heavier-goods flow like <a href="/image-resizer-for-wayfair/">the Wayfair supplier preset</a>, and keep weight-only cleanups on <a href="/image-compress/">Image Compress</a> so you never force a crop you did not intend.</p>
<p>Under the hood the page paints your bitmap onto a canvas at the locked dimension, then re-encodes to JPEG. That step is lossy by design — archive your layered originals elsewhere, because the portal only ever needs the flattened selling copy.</p>
<p>Leave a small safe zone inside the square. The catalog sometimes applies its own secondary trim for thumbnails, and a subject jammed against the edge can lose a corner in the grid view.</p>
<p>The color assumption is sRGB. If your capture carries an exotic profile, convert before this pass so the retailer's rendering does not shift your brand reds after ingestion.</p>
<p>Remember, geometry only clears a preventable reject. Titles, price, and guest reviews still drive placement; a clean hero simply opens the gate so the rest of your listing can compete.</p>`,
