    presetH2: "Why 1280×720 at quality 83 balances punch and a tight 2 MB cap",
    presetHtml: `<p>Thumbnails must grab attention as tiny tiles yet stay crisp when expanded, so 1280×720 is the standard 16:9 canvas creators rely on. Quality 83 keeps bold faces and high-contrast text punchy while respecting the platform's genuinely hard 2 MB ceiling — the most demanding limit in this whole set.</p>
<p>Read these as a firm default and confirm Studio's current requirements if the platform ever revises its thumbnail rules.</p>`,
    deepH2: "Click-through, contrast, and squeezing punch under a hard cap",
    deepHtml: `<p>Thumbnails are a creator's storefront; a fraction of viewers ever read the title first. Expressive faces, strong contrast, and a couple of large words drive the click, and all of that has to fit under an unusually strict byte ceiling that punishes careless exports.</p>
<p>Promoting the video elsewhere? Compare the timeline card on <a href="/image-resizer-for-twitter/">the X in-stream preset</a>, and route any pure weight-trimming — like slimming a batch of channel art — through <a href="/image-compress/">Image Compress</a>.</p>
<p>Under the hood the tool paints your frame onto a 1280×720 canvas and re-encodes JPEG at 83. The pass is lossy, so keep the layered thumbnail file in your own project folder and let Studio carry only the flattened image.</p>
<p>Keep faces and text away from the lower-right corner, where the duration stamp overlays the tile. A margin there protects your composition once the player badge lands.</p>
<p>Output is sRGB. Convert any wide-gamut grab first so a vivid backdrop does not shift after the platform ingests the file.</p>
<p>A striking thumbnail only earns the click; retention, the title, and the topic decide whether the video keeps viewers. Use this page so a heavyweight export never blocks your upload at the worst moment.</p>`,
    faqH2: "YouTube thumbnail questions creators ask",
